<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Michele Maggiore, Pavia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Michele Maggiore is a Romanesque basilica in Pavia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, rebuilt in sandstone in the early 12th century on the site of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> church. It was used for royal coronations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> there in 1155. Its façade is covered with carved bands of animals, monsters and figures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-michele-maggiore-pavia.jpg" alt="San Michele Maggiore, Pavia" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Groume · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Michele_crop.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
